    "description": "Read operation. Retrieve all required pages before reporting complete totals. Returns a **paginated** list of credit types.\n\nA credit type defines a kind of credit/points balance (e.g. loyalty points). The response is a standard paginated envelope: the records are in `data[]` (each item is `id`, `name`, `allow_decimal`, `status`, `unit`, `description`, `created`, `modified`, `deleted_at`), alongside pagination metadata (`current_page`, `per_page`, `total`, `last_page`, `from`, `to`, `links`, and the `*_page_url` fields).\n\n| Parameter | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `page` | Page number (default `1`). |\n| `per_page` | Items per page (default `20`). |\n| `filter[<field>][<operator>]` | Filter by a field (see Filterable fields below). |\n| `sort[<field>]` | Sort by a field, `asc` or `desc`. |\n| `fields[]` | Restrict the returned columns. |\n\n**Filterable fields.** Filter with `filter[<field>][<operator>]=<value>`:\n\n| Field | Type | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| `name` | string | `filter[name][like]=Loyalty` |\n| `status` | enum (`0` Inactive, `1` Active) | `filter[status][equal]=1` |\n\nIn practice the endpoint filters and sorts on any existing column. With no `sort`, results default to newest-first (by `id`).\n\nThe trailing path segment sets the data-detail level (e.g. `/credit_type/list/1`); it is optional for this entity and does not change the returned records — it is only echoed back into the pagination URLs.",
